<edge source="65acf9bc-e764-4677-987e-baf89f2e6822" target="8828a9e7-6ada-4747-9993-0ab39713e114">
  <data key="d3">Reinstating a lapsed policy depends on proving insurability, whereas the incontestability clause protects the policyholder’s benefits after two years, illustrating complementary mechanisms governing policy longevity and legal enforceability.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="65acf9bc-e764-4677-987e-baf89f2e6822" target="2b144ac4-5731-4e59-b5d4-9c1044113d9e">
  <data key="d3">Reinstating a lapsed policy depends on providing acceptable evidence of insurability, ensuring the applicant remains eligible despite lapses. This requirement operates independently of the incontestability rule, which only affects claims eligibility after two years; however, reinstatement could precede or follow the incontestability window.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
