  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="db6187a7-0b1a-4b0c-9738-cb192deba2b7">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="243731d2-b797-41b2-9e9d-3ade15f49b31">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">rider</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="03cfef7f-f2a0-44bd-864f-0344e97bc6d0">
  <data key="d3">When the named beneficiary does not survive the insured, the rule reverts to the policy owner as the alternate recipient, relying on the prior specification of beneficiary eligibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured and the policy owner is alive, the death benefit is paid to them; otherwise, it passes to the owner’s estate. This reflects a sequential contingency plan based on who remains after the insured’s passing.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional fallback</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="1c097ac1-210c-471f-87e7-62f4f7ca9887">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying the death benefit to the policy owner if no beneficiary survives relies on the policy owner having authority to designate or update beneficiaries. This ensures the ownership role remains relevant even after the insured’s passing.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="6903f2e1-fcf8-476e-af64-83ccd3093b64">
  <data key="d3">Sentence 1 defines the fallback payment destination when no beneficiary survives, while Sentence 2 establishes that any beneficiary update becomes effective at the time of signing, ensuring timely implementation of such decisions.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="4f081890-55f2-4345-893f-c638987334c2">
  <data key="d3">If no surviving beneficiary exists, the death benefit goes to the policy owner only if they are alive; however, this depends on prior beneficiary designations being properly updated, which occurs instantly upon submission per fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="ea7568f9-97e8-481c-ba9d-673126fb9ca4">
  <data key="d3">If no surviving beneficiary exists, the death benefit goes to the policy owner only if they are alive; however, any prior beneficiary update remains binding due to its immediate生效 upon filing, ensuring the intended successor is recognized despite the insured’s demise.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="4ec4c4a4-c570-4478-be1c-c95a2d28b386">
  <data key="d3">If no beneficiary survives the insured, the death benefit goes to the policy owner only if they are alive. This contingency relies on the policy owner having control over beneficiary assignments, allowing them to update or designate new recipients before death.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="f292fa61-3d93-4115-9af6-3725ae88913a">
  <data key="d3">If no beneficiary survives the insured, the death benefit may go to the policy owner; however, this right can be affected by any existing assignment of the policy, potentially restricting the owner’s claim.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="9a4ee5a5-54a6-4cfd-82d7-4f61d67a4610">
  <data key="d3">If no beneficiary survives the insured, the death benefit goes to the policy owner only if they are alive; this outcome relies on prior beneficiary designations being made, which the policy owner controls via their right to request them.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd" target="558f6754-63ec-4df4-bcd2-35ea7c37ab09">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence establishes a fallback condition where the policy owner receives the death benefit if no beneficiary survives. This context supports the second sentence, which explains the policy owner’s authority to determine how the benefit is delivered once it is payable.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
