<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="03cfef7f-f2a0-44bd-864f-0344e97bc6d0">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured and the owner is dead, the outcome relies on prior definitions of beneficiary validity, making it contingent on the initial identification process described in the first statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd">
  <data key="d3">If the policy owner dies before or simultaneously with the insured and no beneficiary survives, the death benefit defaults to the owner’s estate—this scenario logically follows from the prior case where the owner was still alive.</data>
  <data key="d4">complementary condition</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="4f081890-55f2-4345-893f-c638987334c2">
  <data key="d3">This statement defines the fallback distribution path when no eligible beneficiary remains, while the second confirms that beneficiary modifications are binding once submitted, even if the insured dies before processing.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual relationship</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="6903f2e1-fcf8-476e-af64-83ccd3093b64">
  <data key="d3">This sentence defines the outcome if no beneficiary or owner survives, while the second explains when a beneficiary update becomes active. The first sets a fallback condition, whereas the second establishes operational timing for changes.</data>
  <data key="d4">contingency relation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="f0a5245e-9eeb-422b-b2ca-4304e824a7be">
  <data key="d3">This sentence defines the outcome if no beneficiary survives, while the second explains when a beneficiary update becomes active. The first sets a fallback condition, whereas the second establishes the mechanism for updating it.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="f292fa61-3d93-4115-9af6-3725ae88913a">
  <data key="d3">When no surviving beneficiary exists and the policy owner has also passed away, the death benefit defaults to the owner’s estate. This outcome may be affected if the policy was previously assigned, as such assignments can restrict or alter ownership rights.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930" target="9a4ee5a5-54a6-4cfd-82d7-4f61d67a4610">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying the death benefit to the estate relies on whether the policy owner exercised their right to name beneficiaries. Without any designation by the owner, the default occurs.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
